{-
   Every string literal the acceptance table of Implementation-String.md part 1.1 refuses.

   # resolver: ownership

   The rule is one code point that means the same thing as a unit of the encoding the type's width
   names. So an eight-bit type and `CodeUnit` take ASCII alone - `"é" :: U8` would be Latin-1's
   `0xE9` and not the character, and `"é" :: CodeUnit` would be a different number on each target -
   a sixteen-bit type takes one BMP scalar value it can hold, and nothing takes a surrogate or more
   than one character. Each refusal is reported at the literal, once, with no second report that a
   `String` does not fit the position.

   The constant position at a type whose instance is a program's own is `Reject.FromTextDefault.yana`,
   since a declaration's refusal ends resolution before any body is read.
-}

fn main() -> Int:
    let two = "ab" :: Char
    let none = "" :: CodeUnit
    let latin = "é" :: U8
    let unit = "é" :: CodeUnit
    let signed = "é" :: I8
    let astral = "😀" :: U16
    let high = "가" :: I16
    let surrogate = "\xD800" :: Char
    let wide = "\xDFFF" :: U32
    return 0
